<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đinh Tiên Hoàng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">924–979</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Dinh Tien Hoang, Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, Dinh Bo Linh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Đinh Tiên Hoàng, born Đinh Bộ Lĩnh, was a local leader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a> who defeated the rival warlords of the Twelve Warlords period. In 968 he proclaimed himself emperor, named the state Đại Cồ Việt and built a fortified capital at Hoa Lư. He was murdered with his eldest son in 979, and the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a> soon took the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang.jpg" alt="Đinh Tiên Hoàng" width="298" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hungquoc at Vietnamese Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VuaDinhTienHoang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đinh Bộ Lĩnh was born in 924 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a>, south of the Red River Delta in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninh-binh-province">Ninh Bình Province</a>. His real name was allegedly Đinh Hoàn. He grew up in a local village during the disintegration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, which had dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> for centuries, and became a local military leader at a very young age. The first independent Vietnamese polity had emerged when the warlord Ngô Quyền defeated the forces of the Southern Han at the First Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bach-dang-river">Bạch Đằng River</a> in 938, but the Ngô dynasty was weak and could not unify the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Faced with anarchy as twelve warlords competed for control, and with the threat of the Southern Han, which saw itself as heir to the ancient kingdom of Nan Yue, Đinh Bộ Lĩnh sought to unify the Vietnamese. When the last Ngô king died in 965 he seized power and founded a new kingdom with its capital in his home district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a>. To establish his legitimacy he married a woman of the Ngô family. In his first years he avoided antagonising the Southern Han, but in 968 he adopted the title of emperor, Hoàng Đế, declaring independence from Chinese overlordship, and called his kingdom Đại Cồ Việt. He became known as Đinh Tiên Hoàng, the Former Đinh Emperor.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> annexed the Southern Han in 971, he sent a tribute mission to the Song in 972. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Emperor Taizu of Song</a> recognised him as Giao Chỉ Quận Vương, King of Giao Chi, a title that implied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. In exchange for tribute payable every three years, Đinh Bộ Lĩnh obtained a non-aggression agreement. He also reformed the administration and the armed forces, established a royal court with a hierarchy of civil and military officials, and set up a harsh justice system in which treason was punished by cooking in a vat of boiling oil or by being fed to a caged tiger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 979 a palace official, inspired by a dream, killed Đinh Bộ Lĩnh and his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-lien">Đinh Liễn</a> while they slept in the palace courtyard. The general Nguyễn Bặc quickly arrested and executed the killer. The throne passed to the emperor's surviving six-year-old son. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> sent an army to exploit the turmoil, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a>, commander-in-chief of the army, took power, dethroned the child, removed his opponents at court and defeated the Song invasion. He proclaimed himself emperor and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-le-dynasty">Early Lê dynasty</a>, still calling the country Đại Cồ Việt. Đinh Bộ Lĩnh is regarded as a significant figure in the establishment of Vietnamese independence and political unity in the 10th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đinh Bộ Lĩnh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dinh Bo Linh</b>, a local leader from Hoa Lu in present-day Ninh Binh, defeated the rival warlords one by one and in 968 proclaimed himself emperor, Dinh Tien Hoang.</blockquote><a href="/state/dinh-dynasty">Dinh dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 980, as the Song dynasty prepared to invade Dai Co Viet after the murder of <b>Dinh Tien Hoang</b>, the commander-in-chief Le Hoan took the throne with the backing of the queen mother Duong Van Nga.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-le-dynasty">Early Le dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dinh Bo Linh</b> reunified it in 968, founding the Dinh dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/ngo-dynasty">Ngo dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gia Viễn, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Hoa Lư Ancient Capital, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.33,105.88&amp;year=959" aria-label="Gia Viễn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Đinh Tiên Hoàng"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3879 5753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3879 5753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.33,105.88&amp;year=959">What was here in 959 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
